"Furthermore, monks, these amazing verses, never heard before, occurred to Vipassī, the Blessed One, the Worthy One, the perfectly enlightened one:

'With difficulty I have attained (this), enough of proclaiming (it) now;
This Dhamma is not easily understood by those overcome with lust and hatred.

'Going against the stream, subtle, deep, hard to see, minute;
Those dyed in lust will not see (it), shrouded in a mass of darkness.'

"Thus, monks, as the Lord Buddha Vipassī pondered, his mind was inclined to inaction rather than to teaching the Dhamma.

And when the Blessed One attained final emancipation (*parinibbāna*), simultaneously with the *parinibbāna*, there was a great earthquake (*mahābhūmicālo*), terrible (*bhiṃsanako*) and hair-raising (*salomahaṃso*).

And the divine drums (*devadundubhiyo*) resounded.

When the Blessed One attained final emancipation (*parinibbāna*), simultaneously with the *parinibbāna*, Brahmā Sahampati uttered this verse:

"All beings will lay down their aggregate in the world;

Where the teacher is such, an unparalleled individual in the world;

The *Tathāgata* who has attained strength, the fully awakened one (*sambuddha*), has attained final emancipation (*parinibbuto*)."

When the Blessed One attained *parinirvāṇa*, at the very moment of *parinirvāṇa*, Śakra, the lord of the gods, uttered this verse:

"Impermanent, alas, are conditioned things, prone to arising and passing away;
Having arisen, they cease; their stilling is bliss."

222. Immediately after the Blessed One (bhagavati) had attained complete extinction (parinibbute), the Venerable Āniruddha (āyasmā anuruddho) uttered these verses (imā gāthāyo abhāsi):

"There was no in-and-out breathing for him, the steadfast one, whose heart was firm (ṭhitacittassa tādino);

The sage (muni), free from craving (anejo), has passed away to peace (santimārabbha), he who made an end of time (yam kālamakarī).

With an unattached (asallīnena) mind, he endured feeling (vedanaṃ ajjhavāsayi);

Like the extinguishing of a lamp (pajjotasseva nibbānaṃ), was the liberation (vimokkho) of his mind (cetaso ahū’ti)."

When the Blessed One (bhagavati) had attained complete extinction (parinibbute), at the complete extinction (parinibbānā), venerable (āyasmā) Ānanda (ānando) uttered this verse (imaṃ gāthaṃ abhāsi):

"Then (tadā) it was frightening (yaṃ bhiṃsanakaṃ), then (tadā) it was hair-raising (lomahaṃsanaṃ);
With all the best qualities, at the extinction (parinibbute) of the Fully Awakened One (sambuddhe)," thus (iti).

237. When this was said, the brahmin Droṇa said this to that assembly, that group:

“Listen, good sirs, to a word from me;

We say the Buddha was patient;

It is not right that to such an excellent person,

there should be any violence to the parts of his body.

All you sirs, united and in harmony,

rejoicing, let us make eight portions;

May the stupas be widespread in the directions,

[so that] many people may be pleased with the one with vision."

339. Defeated by Vajirahattha, the *asura*s dwell in the ocean.

These brothers of Vāsava are powerful and glorious.

"Kālakañcās are greatly frightening, *asura*s who are destroyers of alms;

Vepacitti and Sucitti, and Prahārāda, together with Namucī.

"And a hundred sons of Bali, all named Verocana;

Having gathered Bali's army, they approached Rāhubhadda;

Now is the occasion, sir, of the monks’ forest meeting."

340. The *devas* of water (*āpas*), earth (*pṛthavī*), fire (*tejas*), and wind (*vāyu*) have come here.

The Varuṇas, the Vāruṇas, the *devas*, Soma together with Yasa.

[The *devas*] of loving-kindness (*maitrī*), compassion (*karuṇā*), and body (*kāyikā*) have come, the prestigious *devas*;

These ten tenfold hosts, all of varied hue.

Powerful, effulgent, glamorous, prestigious;

Rejoicing, they approached the assembly (*samiti*) of monks (*bhikkhū*), the forest (*vana*).

The Veṇḍu *devas*, Sahali, Asama, and the two Yamas;

The Canda-sūpanisa *devas*, having placed the moon (*canda*) in front.

The Sūriya-sūpanisa *devas*, having placed the sun (*sūriya*) in front;

Having placed the constellations (*nakṣattrāṇi*) in front, the slow clouds (*mandavalāhakā*) have come.

Vāsava, the best of the Vasus, Śakka has come, Purindada;

These ten tenfold hosts, all of varied hue.

Powerful, effulgent, glamorous, prestigious;

Rejoicing, they approached the assembly of monks, the forest.

Then the Sahabhū *devas* came, like a flame of water and fire;

Ariṣṭhakā, Rojā, and those resembling Umā's flowers.

The Varuṇas, the Sahadhammās, the Accutās, and the Anejakās;

The Sūleyyarucirā have come, the Vāsavanesinās have come;

These ten tenfold hosts, all of varied hue.

Powerful, effulgent, glamorous, prestigious;

Rejoicing, they approached the assembly of monks, the forest.

The Samānās, the Mahāsamanās, the Manusās, the Manusuttamās;

The *devas* corrupted by play (*khiḍḍāpadosikā*) have come, the *devas* corrupted by mind (*manopadosikā*) have come.

Then the Hari *devas* came, and those dwelling in red (*lohitavāsinaḥ*);

The Pāragās, the Mahāpāragās, the prestigious *devas* have come;

These ten tenfold hosts, all of varied hue.

Powerful, effulgent, glamorous, prestigious;

Rejoicing, they approached the assembly of monks, the forest.

